and security.

514
00:20:25,700 --> 00:20:27,533
When I accepted
the woman in me--

515
00:20:27,567 --> 00:20:29,700
--want to meet
your mother, James.

516
00:20:29,733 --> 00:20:32,233
We want to know the woman
who has inspired

517
00:20:32,267 --> 00:20:33,500
this splendid essay.

518
00:20:33,533 --> 00:20:34,767
Joan Crawford?

519
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:36,767
The committee of mothers
is meeting here tomorrow

520
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,000
at 11:00.

521
00:20:38,033 --> 00:20:39,667
- Oh, it's b.Y.O.M.
- To discuss plans

522
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:41,967
for the graduation
exercises.

523
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,333
And we'd love
to have your mother

524
00:20:43,367 --> 00:20:45,167
serve on the committee,
James.

525
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:47,533
I don't have a mom,
my dog ate her.

526
00:20:47,567 --> 00:20:49,567
Very well, James.

527
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:51,067
Oh boy, oh boy,
I won, I won!

528
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:52,467
After 11 years
in high school,

529
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:54,233
I finally won!...Something.

530
00:20:54,267 --> 00:20:56,300
- Hey, everybody
lives in that house.
- Yeah.

531
00:20:56,333 --> 00:20:58,133
Mom! Mom!

532
00:20:58,167 --> 00:20:59,400
Oh, mom--

533
00:20:59,433 --> 00:21:02,300
oh no, mom's
in the bottle again!

534
00:21:02,333 --> 00:21:05,500
Eh, looks like mom invited
Joe e. Lewis over again.

535
00:21:07,667 --> 00:21:10,000
Well, she tidied up
the place.

536
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:16,367
Hmm.
"Went to store,

537
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,700
"scotch in fridge.
Love, mom."

538
00:21:20,833 --> 00:21:23,667
"Buy yourself
another mother."

539
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:25,767
It says "p.S. Say yes

540
00:21:25,800 --> 00:21:28,533
to Martini and rossi
on the rocks-- say yes."

541
00:21:30,133 --> 00:21:31,833
Didn't cross her t.

542
00:21:31,867 --> 00:21:33,667
Jeez, her handwriting
is terrible!

543
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,367
She drank all the gin.

544
00:21:44,633 --> 00:21:46,633
Huh, ironically,
the humorful part

545
00:21:46,667 --> 00:21:48,400
is he buys liquor
with the ten bucks.

546
00:21:48,433 --> 00:21:50,800
- D'oh!
- He does.

547
00:21:50,833 --> 00:21:53,167
Where do I start?
It all looks so good.

548
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:56,533
The vodka clashes with
the coffee table.

549
00:21:56,567 --> 00:21:57,833
Mom is hot!

550
00:21:57,867 --> 00:21:59,433
- [Bots growl]
- Hello, Jimmy.

551
00:21:59,467 --> 00:22:01,533
- Oh, hello, Shirley.
- Is your mother home?

552
00:22:01,567 --> 00:22:03,033
- No, she isn't.
- Oh, never mind.

553
00:22:03,067 --> 00:22:04,867
I didn't want
to see her especially.

554
00:22:04,900 --> 00:22:06,267
Besides, I can
see her later.

555
00:22:06,300 --> 00:22:07,800
Okay, who are you?

556
00:22:07,833 --> 00:22:10,167
You can offer me a drink
if you wanna.

557
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:11,433
And I think you wanna.

558
00:22:11,467 --> 00:22:12,600
Sure, help yourself.

559
00:22:12,633 --> 00:22:14,533
- Just pick a table.
- Thanks.

560
00:22:14,567 --> 00:22:17,200
It's a booze progressive
dinner, here.

561
00:22:17,233 --> 00:22:18,533
Well, how about you?

562
00:22:18,567 --> 00:22:19,933
Come on, join me.

563
00:22:19,967 --> 00:22:21,400
Can't do any more
than kill you.

564
00:22:21,433 --> 00:22:22,833
No thanks, not for me.

565
00:22:22,867 --> 00:22:24,667
I've been hitting it
too hard, lately.

566
00:22:24,700 --> 00:22:26,267
Yeah, I know,
it's kind of tough

567
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:27,667
when it gets
the best of you.

568
00:22:27,700 --> 00:22:30,333
Well, here goes
for both of US.

569
00:22:30,367 --> 00:22:33,267
Hey, that's my booze.

570
00:22:33,300 --> 00:22:35,567
Looks like you're having
yourselves a time, you too.

571
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:36,967
Suddenly,
the thin man enters.

572
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:38,267
What goes on here?

573
00:22:38,300 --> 00:22:39,833
This is my first.

574
00:22:39,867 --> 00:22:41,833
- I wouldn't know about Jim.
- Beam.

575
00:22:41,867 --> 00:22:44,000
- Mother home yet?
- No.

576
00:22:44,033 --> 00:22:45,767
Means another cold supper,
I suppose.

577
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:47,567
Vodka sandwiches.

578
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:49,633
What can you expect?
Nine times out of ten,

579
00:22:49,667 --> 00:22:51,833
if she did stay home
all day cooking,

580
00:22:51,867 --> 00:22:53,800
you'd phone the last minute
you had a business date

581
00:22:53,833 --> 00:22:55,500
- and couldn't make it.
- You the Greek chorus?

582
00:22:55,533 --> 00:22:57,800
You women certainly
stick together, don't you?

583
00:22:57,833 --> 00:22:59,800
Not necessarily.

584
00:22:59,833 --> 00:23:01,933
I'd rather stick
to an attractive man.

585
00:23:01,967 --> 00:23:03,733
Dad, do you mind?

586
00:23:03,767 --> 00:23:06,667
Whoop, warden.

587
00:23:06,700 --> 00:23:08,400
Home so early, Dan?

588
00:23:08,433 --> 00:23:10,100
I was a half hour late.

589
00:23:10,133 --> 00:23:12,300
But I was only detained
on business.

590
00:23:12,333 --> 00:23:14,700
You probably had
more important things to do.

591
00:23:14,733 --> 00:23:16,033
You needn't be
so cross about it.

592
00:23:16,067 --> 00:23:17,533
I'm sorry if I'm late.

593
00:23:17,567 --> 00:23:20,067
But the buses are so crowded,
and I couldn't get a taxi.

594
00:23:20,100 --> 00:23:22,033
I suppose you couldn't've
tried an hour earlier.

595
00:23:22,067 --> 00:23:23,400
No, I couldn't.

596
00:23:23,433 --> 00:23:25,500
Fix mother a drink, James.
I'm-- I'm exhausted.

597
00:23:25,533 --> 00:23:26,833
From drinking so much.

598
00:23:26,867 --> 00:23:28,533
Are we gonna have
any dinner tonight?

599
00:23:28,567 --> 00:23:30,467
In a minute,
it's all in the ice box.

600
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:32,800
Hey, could somebody please
do an intervention?

601
00:23:32,833 --> 00:23:34,367
- --Shirley?
- Oh, no thanks.

602
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:35,600
I have a date.

603
00:23:35,633 --> 00:23:38,000
- Another time, then.
- Thanks.

604
00:23:38,033 --> 00:23:39,567
- Ready for our date?
- Well,

605
00:23:39,600 --> 00:23:41,433
may the best man win.

606
00:23:41,467 --> 00:23:43,033
Who is that?

607
00:23:43,067 --> 00:23:44,633
I've had just about
enough of this.

608
00:23:44,667 --> 00:23:46,133
I won't put up
with it any longer.

609
00:23:46,167 --> 00:23:48,467
- What're you talking about?
- The whole setup!

610
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:50,033
No decent meals on time.

611
00:23:50,067 --> 00:23:51,467
The house is always
in a mess.

612
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,333
While you're gadding about,
making a fool of yourself.

613
00:23:53,367 --> 00:23:54,733
- I got an "a"--
- you do what you please.

614
00:23:54,767 --> 00:23:56,000
Why shouldn't I do
what I please?

615
00:23:56,033 --> 00:23:57,467
- That's okay with me.
- The teacher said--

616
00:23:57,500 --> 00:23:59,267
but you're not going
to do it while you're my wife.

617
00:23:59,300 --> 00:24:00,733
That suits me.

618
00:24:00,767 --> 00:24:02,467
You don't suppose I like
going on like this, do you?

619
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:04,167
I'd've divorced you years ago
if it hadn't been for Jimmy.

620
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:05,500
Mother, dad, please.

621
00:24:05,533 --> 00:24:07,267
A flood of help
you've been to him.

622
00:24:07,300 --> 00:24:09,133
A fine home you've
made for him.

623
00:24:09,167 --> 00:24:10,467
- But I got a--
- well, how about
the example

624
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:12,600
you've set him?
Out gambling every night.

625
00:24:12,633 --> 00:24:14,333
Sometimes not even
coming home at all.

626
00:24:14,367 --> 00:24:16,267
- Really good paper--
- a man has to do something
to pass the time.

627
00:24:16,300 --> 00:24:18,367
- Teacher said I was--

628
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,367
- then why do you come home?
- Maybe I won't in the future.

629
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:21,967
- But I can't--
- dad.

630
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:23,333
- Freshen your drink?
- I've got something
to say to you.

631
00:24:23,367 --> 00:24:24,700
- Yeah son?
- It's something
about school.

632
00:24:24,733 --> 00:24:26,600
I won the essay contest,
and the principal said--

633
00:24:26,633 --> 00:24:28,767
well, say, that's fine.

634
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:31,033
Here's $5.
Go out and celebrate.

635
00:24:31,067 --> 00:24:32,833
Spend it on
loose leaf paper.

636
00:24:32,867 --> 00:24:35,333
There goes a great man.

637
00:24:35,367 --> 00:24:37,700
What about my $5?

638
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:40,133
Oh, jeez.

639
00:24:40,167 --> 00:24:41,700
This guy's made
more money today

640
00:24:41,733 --> 00:24:43,233
than I did all the way
through high school.

641
00:24:43,267 --> 00:24:45,067
- He'll be back.
- With another woman.

642
00:24:45,100 --> 00:24:47,333
I don't care
if he comes back or not.

643
00:24:47,367 --> 00:24:48,533
Won't be any different
if he does.

644
00:24:48,567 --> 00:24:49,800
Well, maybe it will.

645
00:24:49,833 --> 00:24:51,200
You see, I didn't tell dad
all that happened

646
00:24:51,233 --> 00:24:52,333
at school today.

647
00:24:52,367 --> 00:24:53,467
The principal
wants you to meet with

648
00:24:53,500 --> 00:24:55,700
the parents graduation committee
tomorrow at 11.

649
00:24:55,733 --> 00:24:58,000
Mrs. Carlyle and Mrs. Whitney
are gonna be there.

650
00:24:58,033 --> 00:24:59,700
All the most important women
in the neighborhood.

651
00:24:59,733 --> 00:25:01,600
- Is there a bar?
- Oh, Jimmy.

652
00:25:01,633 --> 00:25:03,233
--And they really
want me to be there?

653
00:25:03,267 --> 00:25:05,567
Oh, sure they do,
and I want you to go, mom.

654
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:08,533
Yeah, they need
a laughingstock.

655
00:25:08,567 --> 00:25:10,867
I could wear my new
afternoon dress.

656
00:25:10,900 --> 00:25:12,733
And that hat I just bought.

657
00:25:12,767 --> 00:25:14,967
Oh, wait till he gets
the bill for that hat.

658
00:25:23,600 --> 00:25:25,600
Okay, so, is everybody done

659
00:25:25,633 --> 00:25:27,267
with their
art therapy project?

660
00:25:27,300 --> 00:25:28,533
Joel, what's the point

661
00:25:28,567 --> 00:25:30,233
of this art therapy stuff
anyway, huh?

662
00:25:30,267 --> 00:25:31,967
Well, by having you
draw pictures

663
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,000
of your idealized family,
maybe you can escape

664
00:25:35,033 --> 00:25:37,167
some of the deep
psychological problems

665
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:39,067
that Jimmy,
the star of today's movie,

666
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:40,433
suffer because
of his family.

667
00:25:40,467 --> 00:25:41,967
So let's see what
you've drawn, okay?

668
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:43,267
This is crow's.

669
00:25:43,300 --> 00:25:45,300
That's my dad.
He's all-powerful.

670
00:25:45,333 --> 00:25:47,133
His hands are made
of stainless steel,

671
00:25:47,167 --> 00:25:48,333
and he has lasers that

672
00:25:48,367 --> 00:25:50,200
shoot out of his chest.
Pew-pew!

673
00:25:50,233 --> 00:25:51,833
I don't have to tell you
he's the coolest dad

674
00:25:51,867 --> 00:25:53,067
in the whole neighborhood.

675
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:55,133
And we-- when we go
to father-son picnics,

676
00:25:55,167 --> 00:25:56,733
we win every event.

677
00:25:56,767 --> 00:25:58,433
And he dispenses
homespun wisdom,

678
00:25:58,467 --> 00:26:00,333
and teaches
solid midwestern values

679
00:26:00,367 --> 00:26:02,033
while crushing all
who block his path.

680
00:26:02,067 --> 00:26:05,067
Uh-huh, what about
the handlebar mustache?

681
00:26:05,100 --> 00:26:06,200
I don't know.

682
00:26:06,233 --> 00:26:07,767
O-Kay.

683
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:09,067
Let's see here.

684
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:12,733
Uh, oral obsession
with mustache

685
00:26:12,767 --> 00:26:15,933
indicates
nasal-labial shame.

686
00:26:15,967 --> 00:26:17,467
- Sounds serious.
- Okay.

687
00:26:17,500 --> 00:26:19,700
Now let's see yours here.
Tom, what's this?

688
00:26:19,733 --> 00:26:22,300
Okey-doke, uh,
that's my mom, my dad,

689
00:26:22,333 --> 00:26:24,667
and my mom.
My mom is Hayley mills,

690
00:26:24,700 --> 00:26:27,667
my dad is gigantor,
and my mom is Peggy cass.

691
00:26:27,700 --> 00:26:31,433
Uh-huh, and why are your moms
holding hands, Tom?

692
00:26:31,467 --> 00:26:32,533
I don't know.

693
00:26:32,567 --> 00:26:33,967
O-Kay.

694
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:37,533
Let's see, latent
parent-trap syndrome.

695
00:26:37,567 --> 00:26:39,467
Man should be
in a straitjacket.

696
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:41,633
Okay, gypsy, well,
this one's really nice.

697
00:26:41,667 --> 00:26:42,900
What's this one?

698
00:26:42,933 --> 00:26:45,767
Joel, my ideal family
is right here.

699
00:26:45,800 --> 00:26:48,500
And I know Richard basehart
watches over US all.

700
00:26:48,533 --> 00:26:49,733
- Huh.
- Oh...

701
00:26:49,767 --> 00:26:51,767
That's really sweet, gypsy,
but tell me--

702
00:26:51,800 --> 00:26:54,100
why does Richard basehart
get to play god?

703
00:26:54,133 --> 00:26:55,367
I don't know.

704
00:26:55,400 --> 00:26:56,800
Joel, why are you
spending your time

705
00:26:56,833 --> 00:26:58,167
psychoanalyzing robots?

706
00:26:58,200 --> 00:27:00,567
Um... I don't know.

707
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:02,400
I'm kidding, of course.
We'll be right back.

708
00:27:02,433 --> 00:27:04,200
I do know.
I really do know.

709
00:27:11,300 --> 00:27:13,267
[Dan laughs]

710
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:14,533
What? What's so funny?

711
00:27:14,567 --> 00:27:15,833
That Joe bolster.

712
00:27:15,867 --> 00:27:17,100
He just cracks me up.

713
00:27:17,133 --> 00:27:18,967
The mirror
ought to tell you.

714
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,933
I'm getting ready
to go to Jim's school.

715
00:27:20,967 --> 00:27:23,367
Well, you certainly
look the part.

716
00:27:23,400 --> 00:27:25,800
Why don't you act your age?

717
00:27:25,833 --> 00:27:27,433
Stop criticizing me.

718
00:27:27,467 --> 00:27:29,600
You don't like my clothes.
You don't like my hat.

719
00:27:29,633 --> 00:27:30,867
You don't like it
if I sit around here.

720
00:27:30,900 --> 00:27:32,133
You don't like it
if I go out.

721
00:27:32,167 --> 00:27:34,033
Oh, let's stop
this ridiculous farce.

722
00:27:34,067 --> 00:27:35,200
Why don't we quit?

723
00:27:35,233 --> 00:27:37,167
- I-- I like your hat.
- Great idea.

724
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:38,800
Thanks for the suggestion.

725
00:27:38,833 --> 00:27:41,767
So they're divorced now?
That was easy.

726
00:27:41,800 --> 00:27:43,367
Hmm, he's a good husband.

727
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:46,000
He just has a hard time
expressing his feelings.

728
00:27:47,867 --> 00:27:51,833
She's got a sea anemone
on her head.

729
00:27:51,867 --> 00:27:54,567
* mirror mirror
on the wall *

730
00:27:54,600 --> 00:27:56,533
can I make it to last call?

731
00:27:58,800 --> 00:28:00,433
I'm gonna work on
the decorating committee.

732
00:28:00,467 --> 00:28:01,700
Oh, isn't that swell?

733
00:28:01,733 --> 00:28:03,467
Some of US girls thought
we could bring some flowers.

734
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:05,300
I'm gonna build a blimp
and fight the Nazis.

735
00:28:05,333 --> 00:28:06,667
--To win the contest.

736
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:09,200
I think he should read it at
the graduation exercises.

737
00:28:09,233 --> 00:28:10,633
Why, yes, what
an excellent idea.

738
00:28:10,667 --> 00:28:12,067
Wow, the whole school
seems to be

739
00:28:12,100 --> 00:28:13,533
buzzing about that essay.

740
00:28:13,567 --> 00:28:14,733
--My home, that's all.

741
00:28:14,767 --> 00:28:16,400
It's the way
any other fella's is.

742
00:28:16,433 --> 00:28:17,667
The way a home ought to be.

743
00:28:17,700 --> 00:28:19,267
A very happy home,
obviously.

744
00:28:19,300 --> 00:28:21,067
I'm most anxious
to meet your mother.

745
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:22,233
Oh, mom's swell.

746
00:28:22,267 --> 00:28:25,067
[All]
* liar! Liar! Liar!

747
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:26,933
--I had a lot
of brothers and sisters.

748
00:28:26,967 --> 00:28:28,933
She must take
a great interest in her home.

749
00:28:28,967 --> 00:28:30,367
She does. That's all
she thinks about.

750
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:31,900
Oh, that and twist-off caps.

751
00:28:31,933 --> 00:28:34,067
What's more important when
you come right down to it?

752
00:28:34,100 --> 00:28:36,300
A woman's job is her home
and family, isn't it?

753
00:28:36,333 --> 00:28:38,067
At least, that's the way
my mom figures.

754
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:40,333
Mrs.- hello everybody.
- Oh, and did I tell you
she drinks?

755
00:28:40,367 --> 00:28:41,933
Sorry if I'm late.

756
00:28:41,967 --> 00:28:44,433
Miss reardon drinks a lot.

757
00:28:44,467 --> 00:28:46,467
I'm Jimmy Wilson's mother.

758
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,933
- [Giggles] She's drunk.
- It's funny!

759
00:28:48,967 --> 00:28:50,367
How shocking.

760
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:52,167
Eleanor Roosevelt's pissed.

761
00:28:52,200 --> 00:28:54,500
- Ooh.
- How terrible.

762
00:28:54,533 --> 00:28:55,733
- How dreadf--
- mom!

763
00:28:55,767 --> 00:28:57,000
Did you bring another glass?

764
00:28:57,033 --> 00:28:59,033
